#table-section[Centers and Linearizers of Simply Connected Real Simple Lie
  Groups] <tab:centers-and-linearizers>
#source(333)Denote by $frak(g)$ a noncompact real simple Lie algebra that does
not admit a complex structure, $G$ the corresponding simply connected Lie group.
Denote by $cyclic(Z, m)$ the cyclic group of order $m = 2, 3, ..., oo$ with
generator $Z$. In the column "Generators" representatives of the generators of
$Z(G)$ in the lattice $P^or (Delta_(frak(g)(CC))) inter frak(k)(CC)$ (see
Theorem~@th:real-semisimple-center-fundamental-group) are listed. In the fifth
column the group $G_"lin" = G\/Lambda(G)$ is given (for classical $frak(g)$);
here $Spin_(p, q)$ denotes the connected real form of the group
$Spin_(p+q) (CC)$ (see exercises to §~@sec:existence-uniqueness-theorems)
corresponding to the real form $frak(s o)_(p, q)$ of $frak(s o)_(p+q) (CC)$. In
the column "$b_0$" indicated is a representative of an element $b_0 in Z(G)$
with the property $R(b_0) = epsilon(d R) id$, where $R$ is an irreducible
complex representation of $G$ such that $overline(d R) tilde.op d R$ (see
@ss:representations-real-semisimple[°]).#ed-note[In the row
  $frak(s o)_(2p, 2(l-p)+1)$ the book admits $p = l$. For $frak(s o)_(2l, 1)$,
  however, $Z(G) = cyclic(Z_2, 2)$ with $Z_2 = h_l\/2$, $Lambda(G) = {e}$,
  $G_"lin" = Spin_(2l, 1)$ and $b_0 = (l + l(l + 1)\/2) Z_2$
  (Theorems~@th:real-semisimple-center-fundamental-group and
  @th:linearizer-via-characteristic-lattice). The table also omits
  $frak(s o)_(1, 2l-1)$, $l >= 4$: there $Z(G) = cyclic(Z_2, 2)$ with
  $Z_2 = (h_(l-1) + h_l)\/2$, $Lambda(G) = {e}$, $G_"lin" = Spin_(1, 2l-1)$ and
  $b_0 = ((l - 1)(l - 2)\/2) Z_2$.]

